> On Feb 16, 2015, at 1:10 AM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> I’m trying to finalize a pattern/refactorization of the code that will admit
> partial inits and a few other features we’ll need going forward.
>
> The first issue is maps to and from the unconstrained space.  Right
> now they’re all exposed via stan::io::writer (maps constrained to
> unconstrained) and stan::io::reader (maps unconstrained to
> constrained).  These are classes with an internal value
> accumulator — we’ll need to break this up somehow so that
> we can do the maps one parameter (or set of parameters) at
> a time.  I’m thinking either individual functions or functors,
> either taking in vectors and returning vectors.  Thoughts?

The io::reader and io::writer classes don't know anything about
any specific Stan program, right?  When given data, they provide
pull-like methods to get variables.  The internal pointers they
maintain say where the next data to grab is (at least for the reader).

Are you thinking of modifying the code generated for a model
class to provide a maping from variable names to dimensions and
constraint type?  That seems generally useful to me.

Then we could map the constraint type to the functors to do
the transforms and inverse transforms (with and without the
Jacobians in the case of the inverse transforms).

> For partial inits we have the problem that the model and the
> var_context are in some sense too abstracted and there’s no
> easy way for the var_context to access the constrained-to-
> unconstrained maps.  In his pull requrest, Jiqiang got around
> this by having an external object that stored all of the model’s
> parameters and their sizes but that adds a bunch of indirection.
> I have two proposals.
>
> Firstly, let’s review how initialization currently works.  The
> pattern is vaguely
>
> model::initialize(var_context) {
>  for parameter in parameters {
>    var_context::does_parameter_exist(parameter);
>    values = var_context::get_values(parameter):
>    var_context::verify_dimensions(parameter, dims);
>    internal_parameters.append(map_to_unconstrained(values));
>  }
> }
>
> verify_dimensions will throw an exception if it fails, but the model
> does not explicitly catch this.  An immediate simplification is to
> combine the parameter check, dimension verification, and get
> all into one function that throws an informative exception.

That seems like a very good way to go.  It encapsulates what needs
to be encapsulated and takes some burden off the code generator.

> Now there are two ways of doing random inits without needed
> to figure out the global state of the model.  One option is to
> encode random inits directly into the model,
>
> model::initialize(var_context) {
>  for parameter in parameters {
>    try {
>      values = var_context::get_values(parameter, constrained_dim);  // combined check, verification, and get
>      internal_parameters.append(map_to_unconstrained(values));
>    } catch(var_context_exception) {
>      internal_parameters.append(var_context.random_array(unconstrained_dim));
>    }
>  }
> }

It's bad news to use try/catch for normal courses
of execution.  It makes the code both inefficient (exception
dispatching and catching is expensive because it needs to be
general enough to blow through arbitrary functions) and
inscrutable (exceptions aren't really exceptions).

I like the Javascript (or Java properties) way of specifying
this kind of thing that lets you query whether something is available
from the primary resource (specified inits), and if not, get it
from a secondary resource (random inits).

I don't see why this would be encoded in the model --- which
bits are specific to the model?

> The other option is to have the model pass transformation functors
> to the var_context,
>
> model::initialize(var_context) {
>  for parameter in parameters {
>    internal_parameters.append(var_context.get_unconstrained<map>(parameter, constrained_dim));
>  }
> }
>
> where
>
> template <Map>
> vector<double> var_context::get_unconstrained(parameter, constrained_dim) {
>  if(does_parameter_exist(parameter) && verify_dimensions(parameter, constrained_dim))
>    return Map::unconstrain(get(parameter));
>  else
>    return random_array(Map::unconstrained_dim());
> }

I think I like this second approach better.  Basically, the
compiled program class makes a method available to map variable
names to types (including constraints).  Then the data determines
the sizes.  Then when we init, we use those two things.

Also, I think we'll need to pass in bounds for the random_array if
we want to do anything other than uniform on (-2,2).

Marcus had suggested generalizing beyond uniform at one point,
but it's never seemed useful to me.   But maybe I'm forgetting
some motivation.

By the way, another alternative, also suggested by Marcus,
would be to have a block in the Stan program that specifies
inits.  It'd be nice to have the design be flexible enough that
we can do that at some point if we want.  It'd be nice to keep
the random init code and the model together.  Or maybe not?

> and a Map functor looks like
>
> class map {
>  static vector<double> constrain(vector<double>& input);
>  static vector<double> unconstrained(vector<double>& input);
>  static int constrained_dim();
>  static int unconstrained_dim();
> }
>
> Thoughts?

The issue in my mind is whether we do this all with vectors
or whetner we add more structure.  I don't know which way will play out
to be easier.

If we have some JSON file (or R dump file), then it's going to
define some variables for initialization on the constrained scale.

The sampler is going to need to pull out values for all variables
during initialization.  It makes sense to me to have it first
look in whatever wraps the input file, then if it doesn't find it,
do what you suggest above.

Sorry if this isn't all coherent.  I'm trying to respond so you're
not blocked waiting for comments, but it's late here (I'm not back
to my regular schedule yet!).

- Bob

