> The io::reader and io::writer classes don't know anything about
> any specific Stan program, right?  When given data, they provide
> pull-like methods to get variables.  The internal pointers they
> maintain say where the next data to grab is (at least for the reader).

I’m suggesting break up all of the methods into separate functions
(or functors) so that they can be used for single-parameter transformations
without have to worry about the internal storage which seems unnecessary
to me.

> Are you thinking of modifying the code generated for a model
> class to provide a maping from variable names to dimensions and
> constraint type?  That seems generally useful to me.
>
> Then we could map the constraint type to the functors to do
> the transforms and inverse transforms (with and without the
> Jacobians in the case of the inverse transforms).

That would not be necessary here but it would be useful for MML,
VB, EP, etc.

> It's bad news to use try/catch for normal courses
> of execution.  It makes the code both inefficient (exception
> dispatching and catching is expensive because it needs to be
> general enough to blow through arbitrary functions) and
> inscrutable (exceptions aren't really exceptions).
>
> I like the Javascript (or Java properties) way of specifying
> this kind of thing that lets you query whether something is available
> from the primary resource (specified inits), and if not, get it
> from a secondary resource (random inits).
>
> I don't see why this would be encoded in the model --- which
> bits are specific to the model?

Being able to separate specified inits (which are on the constrained
scale and need to be mapped to the unconstrained scale) and random
inits (which need to happen directly on the unconstrained scale).

> Also, I think we'll need to pass in bounds for the random_array if
> we want to do anything other than uniform on (-2,2).

Of course.  But that all happens in the var_context creation
in the initialization code.

> By the way, another alternative, also suggested by Marcus,
> would be to have a block in the Stan program that specifies
> inits.  It'd be nice to have the design be flexible enough that
> we can do that at some point if we want.  It'd be nice to keep
> the random init code and the model together.  Or maybe not?

What do you you mean the random init code and the model
tougher?  You just argued above to keep them separated.

I think it would be possible to implement that extra block —
we’d just need to create another var_context that takes
in a model instance.

